        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
Neurological adaptation to microgravity is driven by the stimulus rearrangement associated with loss of gravitational loading of the vestibular otolith organs, the proprioceptive organs (muscle spindle, golgi tendon and joint pressure) and the exteroceptive organs (pacinian corpuscles, etc.).  It is also driven by altered biomechanical constraints on body stabilization in space and locomotion through space.  The neurological adaptive response to microgravity is characterized by alterations in CNS integration of sensory information, perceptions of spatial orientation, and motor coordination strategies.  Upon return to gravity following extended stay in microgravity, the lost sensory stimuli and biomechanical contraints return, and a period of readaptation occurs.  During readaptation, crews cannot perform duties and activities requiring keen sensorimotor control or spatial orientation skills on a par with their preflight abilities.  The amplitudes and time constants associated with recovery of preflight performance capabilities are much greater for a long duration flight (>60 days) than for a short duration flight (<20 days).
<P>
APPROACH:<BR>
Evaluation of fitness to return to affected duties was assessed quantitatively using performance measures obtained from the following standardized test batteries:
<BR>
Balance Control (Posture): this test battery provided a quantitative assessment of the ability of crewmembers to fully utilize vestibular, proprioceptive and visual information to estimate spatial orientation  (balance status) and to maintain stable control of upright stance.  At each test session, the subject completed a standard clinical battery of sensory organization tests (SOT) on the Equitest dynamic posturography system.  Each test lasted for 20 seconds, during which the subject maintained a quiet, upright posture with normal, absent, and or mechanically altered visual add proprioceptive sensory inputs.  Test conditions were: standard eyes open Romberg test with support surface fixed; standard eyes open Romberg test with visual surround and support surface fixed; sway-referenced visual surround with fixed support surface; sway-referenced support surface with fixed visual surround; sway-referenced support surface with eyes closed; sway-referenced support surface with sway-referenced visual surround.  Performance assessment was based on the peak body sway during each condition.
<P>
Locomotor Control (Locomotion): tests of dynamic visual acuity (DVA) and impaired modulation of shock wave transmission during locomotion served as global indicators of sensorimotor dysfunction. This test was a two-part test in which subjects first walked along a short overground track and then on a treadmill.  During overground locomotion, the subject gripped a bitebar in the teeth while walking 6-8 meters at a freely chosen pace across a walkway with a built in force plate.  The task was repeated 4-6 times to ensure clean right foot contact with the force plate.  Performance assessment was based on the relative amplitude of the shock wave transmitted from the heel to the head.  During the treadmill locomotion, the subject walked t 6.4 km/hr and focused gaze on a laptop computer screen placed 2 meters in front of the treadmill at eye level.  While walking, the subject read aloud a series of numbers of varying sizes that flashed across the screen.  Performance assessment was based on the number of correct responses to the dynamic visual acuity task.
<P>
Eccentric Gaze-Holding: this task provided a quantitative assessment of stability (state) of the brainstem neural integrator function, and indirectly, of the eye movement control system following space flight.  This test was only performed on  NASA 7.
<P>
The study was conducted preflight and postflight.  Preflight baseline data collection was performed at 60, 30 and 10 days before flight, and postflight collection occurred on landing day (2-3 hours after landing) and on days 1, 2, 4, 8, 12 after landing.  Sessions at 16, 32 and 64 days after landing were conducted if the investigator did not see a return to preflight responses in the preceding sessions.
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
Results of this experiment are not available.
